 ],
 "answer_text": "Found 3 gene records for Li-Fraumeni syndrome: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [3], checkpoint kinase 2 [5] and tumor protein p53 [6], linked to 1 disease: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [4]. \n\nTP53 is the principal gene, encoding the tumor suppressor protein p53, and germline mutations in TP53 cause the syndrome through loss of p53's genome-preserving functions [1]. \n\nThe syndrome follows autosomal dominant inheritance with high penetrance, and affected individuals carry a very high lifetime risk of multiple malignancies including soft tissue sarcomas, osteosarcoma, premenopausal breast cancer, brain tumors, and adrenal cortical carcinoma [1]. Clinical variability is common, and phenotypic heterogeneity occurs even among family members sharing the same pathogenic TP53 variant [2]. \n\nDisease records found\n\nDisease name: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [4]. \n\nGene-to-disease mapping\n\nGene NCBIGene:1029, name: cyclin dependent kinase inhibitor 2A [3]. Gene NCBIGene:11200, name: checkpoint kinase 2 [5]. Gene NCBIGene:7157, name: tumor protein p53 [6]. \n\nPubmed records found\n\nPubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [7]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [12]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [14]. Pubmed title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome heterogeneity [16]. \n\nMedgen records found\n\nMedgen title: Li-Fraumeni syndrome [8]. Medgen clinical_features: MedGen lists no clinical features for Li-Fraumeni syndrome [9]. \n\nLiterature entity records found\n\nLiterature entity name: Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [10]. \n\nClinical trial records found\n\nClinical trial name: Clinical, Genetic, Behavioral, Laboratory and Epidemiologic Characterization of Individuals and Families at High Risk of Breast/Ovarian Cancer [11]. Clinical trial name: Role of p53 Gene in Metabolism Regulation in Patients With Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [13]. Clinical trial name: Characterization of Patients With Uncommon Presentations and/or Uncommon Diseases Associated With the Cardiovascular System [15]. Clinical trial name: Clinical and Genetic Studies of Li-Fraumeni Syndrome [17]. Clinical trial name: LIFSCREEN : Evaluation of Whole Body MRI for Early Detection of Cancers in Subjects With P53 Mutation (Li-Fraumeni Syndrome) [18]. "
